    "teaserText": "The finest victory is the one that costs you the least.",
    "startText": "[drunk angry]\"Sometimes,\" the instructor explained, \"[drunk smart]you win by making the enemy move where you want him to move. [drunk frighten]Sometimes you frighten him. [drunk whisper]Sometimes you make him think he is about to be surrounded. [drunk joking]And sometimes you allow him to discover that he would rather be somewhere else.\"\n\nAnthony considered this.\n\nIt sounded much more sensible than standing in front of a Greek spear and hoping for the best.\n\nThe exercise was simple: [simple]position the men, force the enemy to lose confidence and break his formation. [smart]A rout could achieve what a costly fight might not.\n\nAnthony was pleased to learn that there were circumstances in which making the other fellow run away counted as a victory.",
    "winText": "[surprised] Not a scratch! Anthony decided that 'strategy' was a fine invention.",
    "loseText": "You do not have to kill every enemy to defeat him. Use positioning and pressure to break the formation and force a rout. A battlefield is not a contest to discover which side can produce the most widows.",
